From faf58931539ab84de114e09d64ed6fde55c7ad72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edward Wiles <emmabenzvi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 02:55:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Part II.4: checkpoint backend provenance -- schema bump,
 validated on resume

CMCheckpoint's cm_gradient_backend was previously recorded ONLY in an
unversioned sidecar (<label>_gradient_backend.txt), never validated on
resume. Adds it to the persisted schema and enforces an explicit policy.

Julia-Serialization gotcha discovered live: adding a field to an
existing struct under the SAME type name breaks deserialization of
every prior file (EOFError, not a catchable type error -- confirmed
against a real copy of chain1/delta_0.1/stage_latest.jls) because
Serialization resolves structs by the type name embedded in the file,
not the call-site annotation. Fixed by versioning the type name:
`CMCheckpoint` (schema 1/2) is now permanently frozen as the legacy
read-only layout; `CMCheckpointV3` is the new persisted type.
`load_cm_checkpoint` tries V3 first, falls back to legacy, and upgrades
via `upgrade_schema2` (cm_gradient_backend=:reference, which is
provably correct -- not a guess -- for every schema-2 file that exists,
since :reference was the kwarg's own default throughout schema-2's
entire lifetime).

Policy: resuming under a different cm_gradient_backend than the
checkpoint was written with is a hard error unless the caller passes
allow_backend_switch=true, in which case the switch is written to a
dedicated audit file and the persisted bandwidth_cache (tuned under the
OLD backend's own selection formula) is cleared rather than silently
reused. Base-state/incumbent correctness is backend-independent (Part I
algebra trace + Part II.2's empirical agreement), so this is a policy
choice about auditability, not a correctness requirement.

17/17 tests pass against real short D=20/W=80000 KNITRO runs: same-
backend resume (both directions), cross-backend resume without/with
override, independent cold re-verification of the resumed incumbent,
interruption+resume, and a corrupt-file rejection. Also fixed a Julia
top-level scoping gotcha found while debugging these tests: a variable
assigned ONLY inside a `catch` clause did not reliably persist to the
outer scope in this script-execution mode -- switched to `Ref` mutation
for the two affected test assertions.

